  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations in NAICS 336611, Ship Building and Repairing, stand at $16.1B for FY2026 to date. There were 4,240 awards, and the average award size is $3.8M. Obligations fell 56.6% year over year. Fiscal-year history shows fluctuations: FY21 was $20.7B, FY22 was $22.3B, FY23 was $26.9B, FY24 was $24.2B, and FY25 was $37.2B, before the current FY26 total of $16.1B. The Department of Defense accounts for the largest share of FY2026 to date obligations at $11.6B, followed by the Department of Homeland Security at $4.3B. Other agencies with notable FY2026 to date activity include the Department of Commerce at $147M and the Department of Transportation at $54.2M. Top vendors for FY2026 to date include HUNTINGTON INGALLS INC at $2.7B, HUNTINGTON INGALLS INCORPORATED at $1.9B, and NATIONAL STEEL AND SHIPBUILDING COMPANY at $1.9B. The market to date is concentrated among a handful of large vendors and dominated by defense and homeland security buying.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal spending in ship building and repairing changed recently?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 56.6% year over year, with FY2026 to date at $16.1B. Prior fiscal years were $20.7B in FY21, $22.3B in FY22, $26.9B in FY23, $24.2B in FY24, and $37.2B in FY25. The current-year total is partial and reported fiscal year to date. The average award size in FY2026 to date is $3.8M across 4,240 awards."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the main buyers and suppliers in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer at $11.6B, followed by the Department of Homeland Security at $4.3B. Other buying agencies include the Department of Commerce at $147M and the Department of Transportation at $54.2M. Leading vendors are HUNTINGTON INGALLS INC at $2.7B, HUNTINGTON INGALLS INCORPORATED at $1.9B, and NATIONAL STEEL AND SHIPBUILDING COMPANY at $1.9B."
